{
  "title": "Google founder Sergey Brin found out using Gemini, company's own AI model, on the internal list of tools engineers are banned from using; to kill that one rule 'upset Brin' passed over the 'fight' to company's CEO",
  "publication_date": "2026-07-26T06:06:40Z",
  "summary": "Google co-founder Sergey Brin was reportedly found using Gemini, Google's in-house AI model, despite Gemini appearing on an internal list of tools banned for engineers. Brin sought to remove the rule, was described as \"upset,\" and reportedly passed the dispute to the company CEO. The report raises questions about internal policy and governance.",
  "content": "Google founder Sergey Brin was reportedly found using Gemini, the company's own AI model, even though Gemini appeared on an internal list of tools engineers were banned from using.\n\nAccording to the report, Brin pushed to have that single rule removed. The story says the attempt to \"kill that one rule\" \"upset Brin,\" and that he ultimately passed the \"fight\" over the issue to the company's CEO. The characterization of emotions and the decision to escalate to the chief executive were central details in the coverage.\n\nThe episode underscores tensions that can arise inside large technology companies when internal tool policies, security rules and executive behavior intersect. Lists of approved and banned tools are used to manage risk, compliance and product development practices; when senior executives seek exceptions, it can prompt concerns about consistent enforcement, transparency and governance.\n\nThe item was published by the Times of India Tech Desk, which described itself as a team dedicated to covering technology news, including AI developments, cybersecurity and platform trends. The original piece included contextual reporting on internal policy and the reaction within Google, as summarized above.",
